Move the attribute seam's policy into core

A SOLID/OCP review across all three repos found the seams genuinely
generic -- grep -i "role" across core returns nothing -- with the
policy sitting in the wrong layer.

Everything about setAttributes that is not storage was being decided
inside each transport, independently. `force` is documented as a core
guarantee and the roles plugin's whole permission model rests on it, but
core never checked: a third-party transport could implement the
documented signature exactly, omit the check, and every consumer's
permission model would silently evaporate with nothing to report it.
`undefined` was decided twice and differently -- Postgres deleted the
key, SQLite silently no-opped and returned ok -- so the same call meant
two things depending on what was installed. Non-string values were
documented as unsupported and unenforced, diverging silently rather than
loudly. All three now happen once, in core, before any transport sees
the patch; transports keep their checks as a backstop but the seam doc
says core owns this and they must not relax it.

The roster was the one place core did leak knowledge of its consumer. It
grouped dotted keys and DROPPED their values, which is only correct when
the key carries the fact and the value is bookkeeping -- true of
role.<name>=<timestamp> and nothing else. Applied generically it
destroyed information: machine.host=DESKTOP-20B0940 rendered as
"machine: host", and a plugin could not opt out because this renders
straight into list_relay_agents. The give-away was core's README having
to teach the roles data model ("don't use an empty string as a presence
marker") for a renderer that supposedly interprets nothing. Values are
kept now; grouping stays, since naming a namespace once is structural.

Two contract gaps a third transport could have satisfied literally while
breaking a consumer: listAgents said "peers", but roles requires the
caller's OWN entry and cannot synthesise one because the stored
attributes would be missing; and a transport asserting its own value for
a key (pg does this for machine) had nowhere to be described. Both are
now stated on the seam, as is the register-must-merge rule, which lived
only on the identity seam -- the wrong document for a transport author,
and the failure mode is silent data loss on the second boot.

Finally, config.briefings was a dead aggregate: built, exported,
documented, and consumed by nothing, because entry.mjs composes from the
per-plugin records so it can filter plugins that failed to activate.
Two representations of one fact, where the more obvious one is the bug
this branch exists to prevent.

